WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
INTRO:
Have you ever heard of a company called GE?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.520
You might not know them by name, but I’m sure you&nbsp;
recognize their logo. They’re that company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.200
makes washers, driers, and refrigerators. But,&nbsp;
did you know that they were the world’s largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.360
company not too long ago? In fact, it was just 13&nbsp;
years ago that they reached the top of the Fortune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:29.280
500. Yet today, GE can’t even turn a profit. It’s&nbsp;
actually been 7 years since they were consistently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.560
profitable. This has forced them to liquidate&nbsp;
dozens of subsidaries at rock bottom prices and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.880
reduce their workforce by over a hundred thousand&nbsp;
employees. As recent as 2017, GE boasted over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.920
300,000 employees, but today, they only have&nbsp;
about 168,000. All of this has caused GE stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:54.400
to tumble 85% from their peak shedding nearly $400&nbsp;
billion in market cap. Things have gotten so bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.640
that GE resorted to doing a reverse stock split of&nbsp;
1 to 8 just last year. And if you weren't already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.480
convinced that GE was getting destroyed, just&nbsp;
listen to this. GE was the original member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.880
the Dow Jones and they have continuously been&nbsp;
part of the index since 1907. But, in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.520
the Dow Jones kicked out GE. I’m not sure if they&nbsp;
could’ve been insulted anymore than this. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.840
here’s how GE vaporized a multi-hundred billion&nbsp;
fortune before a newborn could become a teenager.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.920
HOUSE OF CARDS:&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.160
While GE’s fundamental crisis didn’t&nbsp;
become evident till the last 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:40.720
GE has actually been making bad moves for over&nbsp;
40 years. It all started in 1981 when a man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.560
Jack Welch became the CEO of the company. Jack&nbsp;
came from a rather humble family. His father was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.280
a regular old railroad conductor while his mother&nbsp;
was a homemaker. This meant that throughout his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.920
childhood, he worked several odd jobs including&nbsp;
being a golf caddie, a newspaper delivery boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.920
a shoe salesman, and a drill press operator.&nbsp;
And when he joined GE in 1960, he was simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.640
a junior chemical engineer. Considering this blue&nbsp;
collar background, you would think that Jack was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.680
sympathetic to the struggles of everyday Americans&nbsp;
and that he would treat his employees really well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:19.760
But Jack was actually the exact opposite. He was&nbsp;
ruthless, brash, and rather heartless. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.160
by the time Jack became a well known businessman,&nbsp;
most people would call him “Neutron Jack”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.680
because he was like a Neutron bomb. But, wait a&nbsp;
minute, what did Jack even do to receive such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.360
title. Well, he was basically the mastermind&nbsp;
behind all of the worst parts of capitalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.800
For example, he was the one that popularized the&nbsp;
“Rank and yank” policy. This is when you fire the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.240
bottom 10% of your workforce every single year.&nbsp;
If this sounds familiar to you, it’s probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.480
because you’ve heard of Amazon adopting the&nbsp;
same policy. While this strategy might sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.760
reasonable at first glance, it leads to terrible&nbsp;
long term performance. Here’s the thing, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.960
you’re constantly firing the bottom 10% of your&nbsp;
workforce, your employees are never able to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.400
the big picture, they’re always worried about&nbsp;
surviving next year’s cuts. This was especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.840
true amongst management which led to cooking&nbsp;
the books. Now, they didn’t do anything illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.800
per se, atleast at the begininng, but they were&nbsp;
manipulating the numbers through every loophole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.720
they could find. For example, lets say you were&nbsp;
the manager of a department at GE and your goal is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.240
to achieve $1,000,000 in profit this quarter. Last&nbsp;
quarter, this department only pulled in $900,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.680
in profit, so you have to increase profits by&nbsp;
roughly 10%. Ideally, you would look for ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.720
to grow the revenue that your department brought&nbsp;
in. But this would take multiple quarters if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.600
years. So instead, you decide to simply fire ten&nbsp;
employees that are being paid a few thousand a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.880
month. This allows your department to beat the&nbsp;
vain metric of $1,000,000 in profit. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:55.040
your department is nowhere near as strong as $1&nbsp;
million department should be. And this wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.640
happening here and there. It was happening company&nbsp;
wide from the top down, and Jack was most infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.840
for it. He didn’t bolster the company’s profits&nbsp;
by firing people, but he did bolster the companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.280
balance sheet by firing people. For example, let’s&nbsp;
say you want to make a billion dollar acquisition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.200
You could wait a few years for the company&nbsp;
to build up sufficient cash reserves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.000
Or you could just fire 5% of your workforce&nbsp;
and use their payrolls to fund the purchase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.280
and that’s exactly what Jack did throughout&nbsp;
his tenure. Jack was only CEO for 20 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:31.840
but within this time, he made 600 acquisitions.&nbsp;
This works out to 30 acquisitions per year or 2.5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.840
acquisitions every single month. Obviously,&nbsp;
this is by no means naturally sustainable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:44.000
Looking back, it’s obvious why GE eventually got&nbsp;
destroyed, but at the time it looked like GE was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.960
crushing it. Given Jack’s strict department wide&nbsp;
goals, the company would always beat Wall Street’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.560
earnings expectations by at least 1 cent per&nbsp;
share. On top of this, their assets were growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.960
exponentially thanks to all of their acquisitions&nbsp;
which sent the company’s stock to the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:06.080
When Jack took over in 1981, the company was only&nbsp;
worth $14 billion, but when he left in 2001, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:13.280
was worth $410 billion. But, Jack didn’t build a&nbsp;
true $410 billion company. What he did was build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.560
machine that could consistently top Wall Street’s&nbsp;
expectations. Also, the company wasn’t filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.960
loyal employees who were excited to build up the&nbsp;
company. Instead, it was basically filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.320
contractors who were good at playing the purging&nbsp;
game. This toxic culture was synonymous with GE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.840
by the time Jack left, and though the company&nbsp;
was doing well on paper, it was fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.000
weaker than ever before. And it just took one&nbsp;
economic downturn to topple the house of cards.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.560
BUBBLE POP:&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.720
By now, I’m sure you’re all familiar with what&nbsp;
happened in 2008: The Great Financial Recession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.600
Now, you might think that GE was relatively&nbsp;
safe from this given that they mainly deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.880
physical products like appliances. So, while sales&nbsp;
could go down, they were in no risk of facing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.960
real crisis right? Well, this would’ve been the&nbsp;
case if GE stuck to their original business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.320
but Jack had expanded GE into basically every&nbsp;
industry you can think of including the financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.520
industry. The financial subsidiary was called GE&nbsp;
Capital and they were the 5th largest lender in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:24.480
the US. Given what happened to lenders in 2008,&nbsp;
this was already a terrible position to be in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.293
but this was just half the story. You see, while&nbsp;
many banks overextended themselves and engaged in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.293 --> 00:06:36.320
subprime lending, atleast they had deposits. They&nbsp;
just lended out these deposits in bad ways. GE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.440
however, didn’t even have deposits. They borrowed&nbsp;
money through commercial papers at low interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:46.000
rates and then lended that money to people at&nbsp;
higher interest rates. GE even bragged about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.000
being the largest borrower of commercial paper. At&nbsp;
the time, they accounted for 4% of all commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.640
paper in the US. For perspective, GE’s entire debt&nbsp;
load translated to over half a trillion dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.520
Personally, I don’t think this was something to&nbsp;
brag about because when the financial crisis hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.320
they were screwed on both ends. They couldn’t&nbsp;
collect payments from borrowers nor could they pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.840
off their own debts. And pretty soon, they needed&nbsp;
a bailout. Now, I know a lot of you guys love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.520
criticize the big banks for getting bailouts of&nbsp;
$10 to $30 billion, but all of that is nothing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:27.440
comparison to what GE got. GE didn’t get a bailout&nbsp;
worth $10, $20, or even $50 billion. Their bailout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:35.920
was worth a disgusting $139 billion. So, if anyone&nbsp;
deserves criticism for bailouts, it’s these guys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.640
This bailout didn’t really help turn around the&nbsp;
company either as the company was already rotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.600
to the core. It basically just ensured that the&nbsp;
company didn’t go under in a matter of weeks.&nbsp;

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.040
THE RECKONING:
Eversince the bailout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:56.800
GE has only been on a decline. Regardless of&nbsp;
what metric you look at, they’ve literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:04.480
only gotten destroyed. Their assets are down from&nbsp;
$781 billion to $185 billion. Their cash reserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:11.440
are down from $120 billion to $20 billion. Their&nbsp;
revenue is down from $150 billion to $75 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:18.880
And as for their net income, well, it’s not even&nbsp;
positive. This isn’t that surprising given that GE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.560
has just been downsizing eversince the bailout.&nbsp;
GE has basically sold everything even including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.680
their appliance business. So, if you bought a&nbsp;
GE appliance in the past 6 years, it wasn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.040
made by GE themselves. It was actually made by a&nbsp;
Chinese company called Haier who has rights to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.800
GE brand. At this point, GE themselves are only&nbsp;
involved in three industries which are aviation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:46.400
healthcare, and energy. And GE is planning on&nbsp;
spinning these off into 3 different companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.040
as well. The healthcare spinoff is&nbsp;
supposed to take place in early 2023&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.720
and the energy spinoff is supposed to take place&nbsp;
in early 2024. This means that the original GE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:02.160
will only be left with the aviation industry.&nbsp;
This is honestly quite a shame given how large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.920
GE was just a decade ago. The only good thing that&nbsp;
has happened to GE over the past decade is their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:14.640
debt has also gone down. Their long term debt&nbsp;
has decreased from $336 billion to $27 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:22.320
Similarly, their total liabilities have decreased&nbsp;
from $656 billion to $150 billion. While this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.760
far better than the position that they used to be&nbsp;
in, it’s still not all that great. GE’s market cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:32.480
currently sits at $80 billion which means that&nbsp;
their total liabilities is still nearly twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:38.400
their market cap. So, GE still has quite a ways&nbsp;
to go. In terms of what GE will look like after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:44.320
the spinoffs, it’s just even more disappointing.&nbsp;
In the past 6 months, GE pulled in total revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:50.000
of $35 billion out of $11.7 billion came from the&nbsp;
Aerospace segment. This means that the Aerospace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.480
segment roughly accounts for a third of their&nbsp;
business. Given that their market cap is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:59.120
$80 billion, once the healthcare and energy&nbsp;
businesses are spun off, their market cap will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:06.160
be closer to $26 billion. And $26 billion in 2022&nbsp;
is equivalent to just $8 billion 1981 dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:13.120
When Jack took over in 1981, the company was worth&nbsp;
$14 billion which means that in just two years, GE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:18.800
will be worth less than what they were worth way&nbsp;
back in 1981. Now, that’s truly quite a tumble.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:22.960
RECOVERY:&nbsp;

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:27.520
Despite GE’s terrible current position, some&nbsp;
people are hopeful that splitting up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.960
focusing on just one industry is exactly what GE&nbsp;
needs. The aviation industry is still very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:38.000
recovering from the pandemic slump, so demand for&nbsp;
aircraft should only go up in the coming years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:43.280
Not to mention, GE’s other segments are starting&nbsp;
to make a turnaround as well. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:48.000
GE Power is finally profitable again and their&nbsp;
focus on renewable energy could pay off big in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.600
coming decades. Similarly, as supply chain issues&nbsp;
finally cool down, GE Healthcare is expected to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:59.280
boast margins in the high teens. So, it looks like&nbsp;
net profitability is actually possible for GE in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:04.880
the coming years. But, don’t let these short term&nbsp;
outlooks fool you. Sure, this could be the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:11.280
for GE, but it’s not like they’re poised to launch&nbsp;
the iPhone or become a leader in AI. Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:15.680
all they’ll be doing is salvaging their existing&nbsp;
businesses and finally making them profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.440
again. In other words, they’re back to ground&nbsp;
zero. Their time at the top isn’t helpful to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:27.520
in any way. If anything, it simply burdened them&nbsp;
with debt that they’re still trying to pay off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:32.080
So, at this point, their chances of recovering&nbsp;
to their former glory is just as good as any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:37.440
other company with their same market cap. In fact,&nbsp;
GE’s chances are probably worse because of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:42.880
remaining debt and lack of momentum. So, what&nbsp;
can we learn from all this? Well, the takeaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:47.200
seems to be that the second a company starts&nbsp;
to value shareholders over customers, they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:51.920
initiated their eventual demise. They might&nbsp;
end up posting good numbers on paper for year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:57.600
or even decades. But their fundamentally flawed&nbsp;
approach does eventually catch up, and by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:03.360
they realize it, it’s already way too late. So, if&nbsp;
you’re a business owner, make sure you don’t put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:09.040
profits over customers because that’s a surefire&nbsp;
way to get screwed, but that’s just what I think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:14.560
Do you think GE deserved their downfall? Comment&nbsp;
